module lawd-criminal-local-rules.local-rules

// The Western District of Louisiana's local criminal rules. Unless the
// court directs otherwise, criminal motions comply with the form
// requirements of Local Civil Rules 7.1, 7.4, 7.5, 7.9, 10.1 and 11.1
// (LCrR 47.1(a)), so LR 7.1's memorandum page limits apply to them.
//
// The local criminal rules set no period of their own: a response to a
// motion is filed "within the delay set by the court" (LCrR 47.1(a)), a
// motion to suppress likewise (LCrR 47.1(b)), and presentencing memoranda
// within the time frame the sentencing judge designates (LCrR 32.2); the
// magistrate judges set the deadlines for motions and responses at
// arraignment (LCrR 59.1(b)(4)). LR 7.4's default civil briefing periods
// (an opposition within 14 days after the motion is filed, a reply within
// seven days after the opposition) govern only where no Notice of Motion
// Setting issues and the judge orders none, while LCrR 47.1(a) leaves the
// criminal response to the court; no criminal default period is encoded.
// The presentence report periods are FRCrimP 32's and stay in lit/frcrimp;
// the probation officer's sentencing recommendation is not disclosed
// (LCrR 32.1(A)), and a presentencing memorandum does not relieve the
// parties of the written objections FRCrimP 32(f)(1) requires (LCrR 32.2).
//
// Conditions that are not deadlines stay in notes: defense counsel review
// the plea package and the Rule 11 colloquy and are prepared at the change
// of plea hearing (LCrR 11.1); a sealed filing needs a motion to seal with
// a proposed order (LCrR 12.1); a Rule 17(c) subpoena motion is filed only
// after conferring and states the documents sought, their relevance and
// admissibility (LCrR 17.1); exhibits are kept until any sentence imposed
// has been fully satisfied (LCrR 55.1). The grand jury premises (LCrR
// 6.1, 6.2), extrajudicial statements (LCrR 53.1 to 53.10), petty offenses
// (LCrR 58.2) and the magistrate judges' duties (LCrR 59.1) state no
// period.
//
// The earlier W.D. La. criminal outline's FRCrimP flow and its decisions
// (United States v. Salerno, Brady v. Maryland, Barker v. Wingo, Zedner v.
// United States, Boykin v. Alabama, Missouri v. Frye, Jackson v. Virginia,
// United States v. Booker, Roe v. Flores-Ortega, Garza v. Idaho) are
// FRCrimP, Speedy Trial Act or constitutional authority and belong to
// lit/frcrimp; they are removed here: no reporter text is vendored.

/// A memorandum in support of or in opposition to a criminal motion: at
/// most 25 pages, exclusive of a table of contents or table of
/// authorities; a judge may grant leave to exceed the limit in an
/// extraordinary case (W.D. La. LCrR 47.1(a); LR 7.1).
limit memorandum {
  pages <= 25 cite "W.D. La. LR 7.1"
} cite "W.D. La. LCrR 47; W.D. La. LR 7.1"

/// A reply memorandum: at most 10 pages (W.D. La. LCrR 47.1(a); LR 7.1).
limit reply-memorandum {
  pages <= 10 cite "W.D. La. LR 7.1"
} cite "W.D. La. LCrR 47; W.D. La. LR 7.1"

/// A criminal motion with its supporting memorandum is filed (W.D. La.
/// LCrR 47.1(a)).
event MotionFiled() paper memorandum
/// A memorandum in opposition to a criminal motion is filed (W.D. La.
/// LCrR 47.1(a)).
event OppositionFiled() paper memorandum
/// A reply memorandum is filed (W.D. La. LCrR 47.1(a); LR 7.1).
event ReplyFiled() paper reply-memorandum
